A regional water-quality oversight team is reviewing whether day-to-day sediment management guidance should differ between catchments influenced mainly by farming activity and those affected more by land development and built-up runoff. Field officers note that cloudier water can create operational concerns for habitat protection, treatment planning, and compliance messaging, although river behaviour is also shaped by channel form, recent site disturbance, vegetation cover, and local drainage patterns. To keep the review practical, the team selected one broadly comparable river stretch from each of ten rural areas and ten urban areas, with no river stretch appearing more than once. The selected stretches were considered similar in size, flow conditions, altitude, and related physical features. The recorded suspended sediment loads (milligrams per litre) were:
\begin{tabular}{lllllllllll} 
Rural & 82 & 55 & 50 & 34 & 50 & 44 & 29 & 77 & 60 & 71 \\
Urban & 95 & 111 & 39 & 78 & 56 & 62 & 82 & 93 & 77 & 62 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

A wildlife protection team is reviewing whether den sites that are harder for passers-by to notice may face different long-term outcomes than sites that are easier to spot from the nearest public access route, such as a road or footpath. For field operations, each site was given a visibility rating on a practical 1-to-6 scale, where 1 means the site could not realistically be seen by members of the public and 6 means it would almost certainly be noticed. The same sites were also classified by current status: either still regularly used by a badger social group or no longer showing recent signs of occupation. The recorded ratings are shown below:

\begin{tabular}{llllllllllllllll} 
Active & 1 & 2 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 2 & 1 & 3 & 5 & 4 & 5 & 4 & 4 & 3 & 5 \\
Unused & 2 & 3 & 1 & 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 3 & 2 & 4 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

